  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:Azarsj8xlBk",
    "purpose": "Demonstrate how to cook a thick bone-in ribeye for two using the reverse sear, explaining the science behind each step and the history of the technique.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "For a big, thick steak, the reverse sear (low oven first, hard sear last) gives the most even edge-to-center doneness and is still Kenji's preferred method.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "One thick steak for two gives a better contrast between crust and medium-rare interior than two thin steaks.",
      "A ribeye is made of the loin muscle and the spinalis (cap); chuck-end ribeyes have more spinalis.",
      "Season with kosher salt, black pepper and optionally MSG (about 5:1 salt to MSG); rest at least 45 minutes or overnight uncovered in the fridge so the salt is reabsorbed.",
      "Surface moisture steals energy from the pan, so dry the steak before searing.",
      "Cook in a low oven (about 200-275°F) to an internal temperature around 120-125°F, then sear in a smoking-hot heavy pan with oil, butter and optional aromatics.",
      "Kenji developed the reverse sear at Cook's Illustrated as a home alternative to sous vide; others like Alton Brown had similar roast recipes earlier.",
      "The steak can sit at room temperature up to about 45 minutes between oven and sear.",
      "A reverse-seared steak needs no resting because its temperature is already even; finish with flaky sea salt since the interior is unseasoned."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Introduction to Ribeye",
        "summary": "Kenji explains why one thick steak for two beats two thin ones and describes the grass-fed bone-in ribeye, its loin and spinalis muscles, and why he prefers chuck-end cuts.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "Seasoning and dry brining",
        "summary": "Seasoning heavily with kosher salt, black pepper and MSG, then explaining how salt draws out and reabsorbs moisture, why surface moisture hurts searing, and resting 45 minutes or overnight.",
        "first_segment": 1,
        "last_segment": 5
      },
      {
        "title": "The reverse sear method",
        "summary": "Inserting a Combustion thermometer, cooking in a low oven to about 125°F, target temperatures for different doneness, and the story of developing the reverse sear at Cook's Illustrated as an alternative to sous vide.",
        "first_segment": 6,
        "last_segment": 11
      },
      {
        "title": "Searing the steak",
        "summary": "Drying the steak and searing it in a heavy cast iron braiser with oil, butter, garlic, shallots and thyme, basting, searing the edges, and noting the steak can wait up to 45 minutes before searing.",
        "first_segment": 11,
        "last_segment": 14
      },
      {
        "title": "Resting and slicing",
        "summary": "Why reverse-seared steak needs no rest, slicing to show even edge-to-edge doneness, finishing with sea salt, and a recap of the method.",
        "first_segment": 14,
        "last_segment": 17
      }
    ],
